---
<BlogPost
  title="Cheapest Dairy Queen Menu Items & Meals (2026)"
  description="The cheapest things at Dairy Queen: treats under $3, food under $5, full meals under $10, a family-of-four plan and the best calories per dollar."
  h1="The Cheapest Things to Order at Dairy Queen"
  crumb="Cheapest DQ Menu"
  eyebrow="Eating cheap at DQ"
  image="/Images/2-for-5-mix-match.webp"
  imageAlt="Dairy Queen 2 for $5 Mix and Match items"
  datePublished="2026-10-21"
  dateModified="2026-10-21"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="lead"><strong>The cheapest thing at Dairy Queen is the DQ Sandwich at about $1.49, and the cheapest full meal is a $4.99 kids meal.</strong> For an adult, the best-value meal is the <a href="/items/7-meal-deal">$7 Meal Deal</a>, and the <a href="/items/2-for-5-mix-match">2 for $5 Mix &amp; Match</a> menu is the cheapest way to get two hot items. Below are the lowest prices in each category, what you can get under $3, $5 and $10, and how to feed four people for about $24.</p>

  <p>All prices here are typical U.S. prices from our own checks. Each franchise sets its own prices, so your store may charge a little more or less.</p>

  <h2 id="by-category">Cheapest Dairy Queen item in each category</h2>
  <p>The table shows the lowest-priced item we track in each part of the menu. Treats are the cheapest by far, and burgers and chicken sandwiches are the most expensive things to order on their own.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Cheapest item</th><th>Typical price</th><th>Calories</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Classic treats</td><td><a href="/items/dq-sandwich">DQ Sandwich</a></td><td class="price-cell">$1.49</td><td>190</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drinks</td><td>Fountain soft drink</td><td class="price-cell">from $2.11</td><td>0–310</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frozen drinks</td><td>Small Misty Slush</td><td class="price-cell">$2.39</td><td>~150</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sides</td><td>Small fries</td><td class="price-cell">$2.49</td><td>260</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blizzards</td><td>Mini Oreo (and other year-round flavors)</td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>~300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shakes</td><td>Small chocolate shake</td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>~710</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chicken</td><td>2-piece chicken strips</td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>~280</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Breakfast</td><td>Breakfast Burrito</td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>380</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hot dogs</td><td>Hot dog</td><td class="price-cell">$3.99</td><td>330</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kids meals</td><td>Any kids meal</td><td class="price-cell">$4.99</td><td>~650–750</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burgers</td><td>Original Cheeseburger</td><td class="price-cell">$6.39</td><td>400</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chicken sandwiches</td><td>Grilled or crispy</td><td class="price-cell">$7.99</td><td>390–470</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strip baskets</td><td>4-piece Chicken Strip Basket</td><td class="price-cell">$9.99</td><td>~1,000</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Two things stand out. First, the Original Cheeseburger costs about $6.39 on its own, only 61 cents less than the whole $7 Meal Deal, so never order it alone. Second, breakfast is served only during breakfast hours at select stores, so the $3.49 burrito is not an option everywhere.</p>

  <h2 id="dollar-menu">Is there a DQ dollar menu?</h2>
  <p>No, not in the sense of items for $1. Nothing we track at Dairy Queen costs a dollar or less. The closest thing to a dollar menu is the 2 for $5 Mix &amp; Match, which works out to $2.50 per item, and a handful of treats that cost under $2: the DQ Sandwich (about $1.49), a small vanilla cone (about $1.79) and the Dilly Bar (about $1.99).</p>

  <h2 id="treats-under-3">Dairy Queen treats under $3</h2>
  <p>You can get a cold treat at any DQ for under $3, including Treat-only stores. These are the options at typical prices:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Treat</th><th>Typical price</th><th>Calories</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>DQ Sandwich</td><td class="price-cell">$1.49</td><td>190</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/items/vanilla-soft-serve-cone">Small vanilla cone</a></td><td class="price-cell">$1.79</td><td>170</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/items/dilly-bar">Dilly Bar</a></td><td class="price-cell">$1.99</td><td>210</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regular vanilla cone</td><td class="price-cell">$2.39</td><td>230</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Small Misty Slush</td><td class="price-cell">$2.39</td><td>~150</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/items/buster-bar">Buster Bar</a></td><td class="price-cell">$2.49</td><td>440</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Non-Dairy Dilly Bar</td><td class="price-cell">$2.49</td><td>220</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large vanilla cone</td><td class="price-cell">$2.99</td><td>340</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Best pick:</strong> the Buster Bar. It costs the same as a Non-Dairy Dilly Bar but has twice the calories, with fudge and peanuts under the chocolate shell. If you are buying for a group of kids, four DQ Sandwiches cost about $5.96, compared with about $7.16 for four small cones and about $7.96 for four Dilly Bars.</p>
  <p>Sundaes just miss the cut. A small hot fudge sundae is about $3.49, and a Mini Blizzard starts at about $3.49 for year-round flavors.</p>

  <h2 id="food-under-5">Dairy Queen food under $5</h2>
  <p>The cheapest hot food at DQ is small fries at about $2.49, and the cheapest entrée is <a href="/items/2pc-chicken-strips">2-piece chicken strips</a> at about $3.49. Here is everything under $5:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Small fries:</strong> about $2.49, 260 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Side salad:</strong> about $3.29, 90 calories without dressing.</li>
    <li><strong>Onion rings (regular):</strong> about $3.29, about 400 calories. Grill &amp; Chill stores only.</li>
    <li><strong>2-piece chicken strips:</strong> about $3.49, about 280 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Breakfast Burrito:</strong> about $3.49, 380 calories (breakfast hours, select stores).</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/items/hot-dog">Hot dog</a>:</strong> about $3.99, 330 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Pretzel sticks with queso:</strong> about $3.99, 440 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Cheese curds:</strong> about $3.99, 470 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Biscuits and gravy:</strong> about $3.99, 400 calories (breakfast hours, select stores).</li>
    <li><strong>Chili cheese dog:</strong> about $4.99, 420 calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Any kids meal:</strong> about $4.99 with an entrée, a side, a drink and a small vanilla cone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The kids meal is the standout. A plain hot dog costs about $3.99, and the <a href="/items/kids-hot-dog-meal">Kids Hot Dog Meal</a> costs about $4.99, so the side, the drink and the cone together cost you about $1 more. DQ does not set an age limit, so adults with a small appetite can order one too. See all three on the <a href="/categories/kids-meals">kids meals menu</a>.</p>
  <p>And for exactly $5, the 2 for $5 menu gets you two items. Pick the ones that cost the most on their own, such as the cheeseburger (about $6.39) and the chili cheese dog (about $4.99). That pair is worth about $11.38 at regular prices, so you save about $6.38. Small fries are the weakest pick because they are already cheap.</p>

  <h2 id="meals-under-10">Full meals under $10</h2>
  <p>The best full meal under $10 is the $7 Meal Deal. It gives you an Original Cheeseburger or 3-piece chicken strips, regular fries, a soft drink and a small sundae. Bought separately at typical prices, the cheeseburger version would cost about $14.48 ($6.39 + $2.49 + $2.11 + $3.49), so you save about $7.48. The deal is offered all day at participating DQ Grill &amp; Chill stores, not at Treat-only stores.</p>
  <p>Here are five ways to eat a full meal for under $10, with the math:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Meal</th><th>How it adds up</th><th>Total</th><th>Calories</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Kids meal (any)</td><td>Entrée, side, drink and cone</td><td class="price-cell">$4.99</td><td>~650–750</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$7 Meal Deal, cheeseburger</td><td>Burger, fries, drink, small sundae</td><td class="price-cell">$7.00</td><td>~900</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$7 Meal Deal, chicken</td><td>3 strips, fries, drink, small sundae</td><td class="price-cell">$7.00</td><td>~1,100</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 for $5 + soft drink</td><td>2-piece strips + pretzel sticks ($5) + drink ($2.11)</td><td class="price-cell">$7.11</td><td>~720 plus drink</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 for $5 + drink + Dilly Bar</td><td>$5 + $2.11 + $1.99</td><td class="price-cell">$9.10</td><td>depends on picks</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>For about $2 more than a 2 for $5 order, the $7 Meal Deal adds a drink and a dessert, so it is the better choice whenever you want a full meal. The 2 for $5 makes more sense for a snack or a light meal. If you want the 3-strip chicken option, the meal deal is also the cheap way into DQ chicken: a 4-piece Chicken Strip Basket on its own is about $9.99 with no drink or dessert. Full details on each bundle are in our <a href="/blog/dq-meal-deals-guide">DQ meal deals guide</a>.</p>
  <p>One more tip: many stores will swap the meal deal’s sundae for a Mini Blizzard for a small extra charge. Ask at the counter, because it depends on the location.</p>

  <h2 id="family-of-four">Cheapest way to feed a family of four</h2>
  <p>The cheapest full dinner for two adults and two kids is <strong>two $7 Meal Deals plus two kids meals, for about $23.98</strong>. Everyone gets an entrée, a side, a drink and a dessert.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two $7 Meal Deals:</strong> 2 × $7.00 = $14.00</li>
    <li><strong>Two kids meals:</strong> 2 × $4.99 = $9.98</li>
    <li><strong>Total:</strong> $14.00 + $9.98 = <strong>$23.98</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>For comparison, four Original Cheeseburgers on their own would cost about $25.56 (4 × $6.39), with no fries, drinks or dessert.</p>
  <p>If the adults are light eaters, <strong>four kids meals cost about $19.96</strong> (4 × $4.99), which saves another $4.02. You give up the full-size burger and adult fries, but everyone still gets a meal and a cone.</p>
  <p>For a snack stop instead of a meal, two 2 for $5 orders ($10) give you four hot items, and four DQ Sandwiches ($5.96) cover dessert, for about $15.96 in total. Add drinks only if you need them, since a soft drink starts at about $2.11 each.</p>

  <h2 id="calories-per-dollar">Best calories per dollar at DQ</h2>
  <p>If you want the most food for your money, the small chocolate shake is the top item we track, at roughly 203 calories per dollar. That is not a health recommendation, just a measure of how filling an order is for the price. Figures below divide calories by typical price:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Price</th><th>Calories</th><th>Calories per $1</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><a href="/items/chocolate-shake">Small chocolate shake</a></td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>~710</td><td>~203</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buster Bar</td><td class="price-cell">$2.49</td><td>440</td><td>~177</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 for $5: cheeseburger + chili cheese dog</td><td class="price-cell">$5.00</td><td>820</td><td>164</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$7 Meal Deal, chicken</td><td class="price-cell">$7.00</td><td>~1,100</td><td>~157</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large <a href="/items/oreo-blizzard">Oreo Blizzard</a></td><td class="price-cell">$5.99</td><td>920</td><td>~154</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kids Chicken Strips Meal</td><td class="price-cell">$4.99</td><td>~750</td><td>~150</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large fries</td><td class="price-cell">$3.49</td><td>480</td><td>~138</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Original Cheeseburger (alone)</td><td class="price-cell">$6.39</td><td>400</td><td>~63</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The pattern is simple. Bundles and bigger sizes give you more per dollar, and single entrées give you the least. A Large Oreo Blizzard costs only 50 cents more than a Medium but adds about 330 calories, so it is a good size to split between two people. The cheeseburger at the bottom of the table is the same burger that comes in the $7 Meal Deal, which is the clearest sign that you should order it as part of the meal. For the lighter end of the menu, see our <a href="/blog/dairy-queen-calories-nutrition-guide">DQ calories guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="save-more">More ways to pay less at DQ</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Check the app first.</strong> The DQ app has a Deals tab with coupons and earns about 10 points per dollar toward free items. Our <a href="/blog/dairy-queen-app-rewards-hacks">DQ app rewards guide</a> explains how to stack a deal and a reward.</li>
    <li><strong>Never buy a burger alone.</strong> Order it in the $7 Meal Deal or on the 2 for $5 menu.</li>
    <li><strong>Share a large.</strong> Large fries and Large Blizzards cost little more than the next size down.</li>
    <li><strong>Skip fries on the 2 for $5.</strong> Choose the items that cost the most on their own.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For every current deal and combo in one place, see our <a href="/combos">Dairy Queen deals page</a>.</p>
</BlogPost>
